# Add these lines to the droplet's existing crontab (crontab -e) alongside
# everything else already there.
#
# TEMPEST_API_KEY has to be set here (or sourced from a file
# publish_tempest.py reads itself) because cron does not load your shell
# profile.
TEMPEST_API_KEY=your_tempest_api_key_here

# Every 5 minutes -- well above the station's own ~1-minute native
# sampling interval, but frequent enough that the published chart reads
# as live for a casual viewer without hammering the Tempest API on every
# render. No fixed issuance schedule to align to here (unlike the
# scheduled-issuance products elsewhere in this repo), so this is a
# polling interval, not a cycle time.

*/5 * * * * /opt/ingalls-weather-scripts/tempest-temp-chart/venv/bin/python3 /opt/ingalls-weather-scripts/tempest-temp-chart/deploy/publish_tempest.py >> /opt/ingalls-weather-scripts/tempest-temp-chart/state/cron.log 2>&1

# Hourly at :10 past, not "once right after midnight Pacific" --
# publish_daily.py is idempotent per Pacific calendar date (tracks the
# last date it rotated for in state/last_rotation_date.txt), so running
# it hourly means whichever tick is first to land after midnight PT does
# that day's rotation+fetch+render, and every other tick that day is a
# fast no-op -- same reasoning as
# columbia-basin-lightning-daily-map/deploy/crontab.example. Picking :10
# past the hour specifically (rather than lightning-daily's own :15) is
# what actually gets this close to "00:10 PT": since every US Pacific DST
# offset from UTC is a whole number of hours (never a half-hour), "any
# UTC hour:10" always lines up with "some Pacific hour:10" too -- DST
# only shifts *which* UTC hour corresponds to Pacific midnight, never the
# minute -- so the tick that lands in the hour after midnight PT always
# fires at exactly 00:10 PT, winter or summer, without needing a DST-
# aware UTC cron time.

10 * * * * /opt/ingalls-weather-scripts/tempest-temp-chart/venv/bin/python3 /opt/ingalls-weather-scripts/tempest-temp-chart/deploy/publish_daily.py >> /opt/ingalls-weather-scripts/tempest-temp-chart/state/cron.log 2>&1
